.TH CVEC_APPEND_ARRAY 3 "2026-09-28" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cvec_append_array \- append a C array to a vector (type-inferred, fatal on failure)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cvector.h>

cvec_append_array(v, arr_ptr, elem_count);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro is a wrapper for cvector_append_array() that calls
ccol_fatal_err() when the append fails, so a caller with no useful
recovery for an allocation failure does not need to test a return value.

The macro copies elem_count elements from arr_ptr onto the end of v in one
bulk copy, growing the capacity one time at most instead of once for each
element. The append is all or nothing: either every element lands in the
vector, or the vector stays exactly as it was.

Because the macro copies the elements byte for byte from arr_ptr, arr_ptr
must point at elements of the declared element type of the vector. Unlike
cvec_push(3), this macro converts no element.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
v           Vector to append to
arr_ptr     Pointer to the first element of the array to copy from. It
            can point at a const array.
elem_count  Number of elements to copy
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program with ccol_fatal_err() when the append fails.
The append fails when the allocation fails, when the new element count is
more than ccol_max_elem_count, when elem_count is large enough to overflow
that count, or when arr_ptr is NULL and elem_count is not zero. It also
fails when arr_ptr aliases the backing buffer of v and elem_count reads
past the end of the reservation of that buffer.
An elem_count of zero succeeds and leaves both the vector and arr_ptr
unchanged, so the macro rejects a NULL arr_ptr only when there is
something to copy.
The macro evaluates elem_count exactly once on every path, the failure
path included, so an expression with a side effect is safe to pass.
arr_ptr can point into the backing store of v, for example a pointer from
cvec_data_ptr(3) or from cvec_at_ptr(3); a capacity growth that this call
starts does not invalidate such a pointer. The source range and the
destination range can also overlap, but the source range must stay inside
the reservation of that buffer.
The capacity grows one time at most for each call, to the next power of
two at or above the new element count, with a floor of 4.
The macro asserts when v is NULL.
To handle a failed append yourself, use cvector_append_array(3) directly;
that function does not stop the program.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
int src[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
cvec_construct(vec, int);
cvec_append_array(vec, src, 5);
// cvec_size(vec) == 5
cvec_destroy(vec);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cvector_append_array (3),
.BR cvec_append_cvec (3),
.BR cvec_reserve (3),
.BR cvec_push (3)
